{% extends "layout" %}

{# One collapsible question. The answer is the body of the {% call %} block. #}
{% macro faq(id, question, open=false) %}
<div class="accordion-item">
    <h3 class="accordion-header">
        <button class="accordion-button{% if not open %} collapsed{% endif %}" type="button"
            data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faq-{{ id }}" aria-expanded="{{ 'true' if open else 'false' }}"
            aria-controls="faq-{{ id }}">
            {{ question }}
        </button>
    </h3>
    <div id="faq-{{ id }}" class="accordion-collapse collapse{% if open %} show{% endif %}">
        <div class="accordion-body">
            {{ caller() }}
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
{% endmacro %}

{% block page %}
<div class="page page-narrow">
    <h1 class="page-title">Frequently asked questions</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">How {{ site_name }} works, what it protects, and what it doesn't.</p>

    <section class="mt-4" aria-labelledby="faq-basics">
        <h2 class="h4 mb-3" id="faq-basics">The basics</h2>
        <div class="accordion">
            {% call faq("what", "What is " ~ site_name ~ "?", open=true) %}
            <p>A pastebin for sharing text and files privately. Your paste is encrypted in your browser before it's
                uploaded, so the server only ever stores data it can't read. You get a link to share; whoever opens
                it can read the paste. There's no account to create.</p>
            <p class="mb-0">You can add a password, an expiry time, a view limit (for example, "burn after one
                read"), a file attachment and syntax highlighting for code.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("who", "Who can read my paste?") %}
            <p class="mb-0">Anyone who has the full link, plus the password if you set one. Treat the link like the
                secret itself: share it only with the people you mean to, and use a view limit or a short expiry for
                anything sensitive.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("cost", "Is it free? Is it open source?") %}
            <p class="mb-0">Yes to both. The code behind this site is <a href="{{ source_url }}" target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer">open source</a> under the MIT licence, so anyone can check how the
                encryption works.</p>
            {% endcall %}
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-5" aria-labelledby="faq-security">
        <h2 class="h4 mb-3" id="faq-security">Security and privacy</h2>
        <div class="accordion">
            {% call faq("how", "How does the encryption work?") %}
            <p>When you create a paste, your browser:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>generates a random 128-bit secret,</li>
                <li>derives an AES-256 key from it (with HKDF-SHA256),</li>
                <li>compresses and encrypts the text, the title, the file and the file's name with AES-256-GCM,
                    using your browser's built-in Web Crypto API, and</li>
                <li>uploads only the encrypted result.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="mb-0">The secret goes into the link after the <code>#</code>, like
                <code>{{ site_url }}/s/Ab3dE5gH#<em>secret</em></code>. Browsers never send the part after
                <code>#</code> to the server, so the server never has the key. When someone opens the link, their
                browser reads the secret and decrypts the paste locally.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("server", "What can the server see?") %}
            <p>The server stores the encrypted paste, including its title and any attachment's name, none of which
                it can read. It also stores some information it needs to work:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>the syntax highlighting choice (just "auto" if you leave it on Auto-detect: the language is
                    guessed in your browser),</li>
                <li>the size of the paste and attachment,</li>
                <li>when it was created and when it expires,</li>
                <li>how many times it has been viewed, and the view limit,</li>
                <li>whether it has a password,</li>
                <li>a fingerprint of a check value your browser derives from the link (and password), so the
                    server can tell that someone has the full link before it sends them the paste. It can't be
                    turned back into the link, the password or the key.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0">Like any website, the server can also see the IP addresses that create and open pastes.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("password", "What does adding a password do?") %}
            <p>Without a password, the link alone is enough to read the paste. With one, the encryption key depends
                on both the link and the password, so someone who gets hold of the link still can't read it. The
                password is never sent to the server; it's stretched with PBKDF2-SHA256 (600,000 rounds) in the
                browser, which makes guessing it slow.</p>
            <p>The server only hands out the encrypted paste to a browser that proves it has both the link and the
                password, so nobody can download it to try passwords at their own pace. A wrong password doesn't use
                up a view, and after repeated wrong attempts the server makes you wait.</p>
            <p class="mb-0">For the best protection, send the password through a different channel from the link,
                for example the link by email and the password by text message. Need a strong one? Use the
                <a href="/tools/password-generator">password generator</a>.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("secure", "How secure is it?") %}
            <p>The encryption itself, AES-256-GCM with a key derived from a 128-bit random secret, is the same class
                of cryptography used to protect banking and messaging. Guessing a link's secret by brute force
                isn't feasible. In practice, the weak points are elsewhere:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>The link.</strong> Anyone who sees it can read the paste, so it's only as private as the
                    channel you send it through. A password protects against this.</li>
                <li><strong>Your devices.</strong> Malware or a malicious browser extension on your device, or the
                    recipient's, can read the paste after it's decrypted.</li>
                <li><strong>Trusting the website.</strong> As with every browser-based encrypted service, the
                    encryption code is delivered by the site itself. A compromised server could send altered code.
                    Because the code is open source, it can be checked.</li>
            </ul>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("recover", "I lost the link or the password. Can you recover my paste?") %}
            <p class="mb-0">No. Only the link holds the key, and the password is never sent to the server, so there's
                nothing to recover it from. This is what keeps the server from reading your pastes.</p>
            {% endcall %}
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-5" aria-labelledby="faq-using">
        <h2 class="h4 mb-3" id="faq-using">Using {{ site_name }}</h2>
        <div class="accordion">
            {% call faq("limits", "How large can a paste be?") %}
            <p class="mb-0">Text can be up to 1 MB, and you can attach one file of up to 25 MB.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("expiry", "How do expiry and view limits work?") %}
            <p>Every paste expires, after anywhere from 10 minutes to 1 year (3 days by default). Expired pastes can't
                be opened and are deleted from the server.</p>
            <p class="mb-0">A view limit caps how many times a paste can be opened; set it to 1 for "burn after
                reading". The server counts a view each time it sends the paste to someone with the full link (and
                the password, if there is one); a broken link or a wrong password doesn't use one. After the last
                allowed view, the paste is deleted from the server straight away. If it has an attachment, only the
                file is kept, for up to an hour, so the last viewer can download it. Leave the limit empty for no
                limit.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("delete", "Can I delete a paste?") %}
            <p class="mb-0">Yes. When you create a paste, the link dialog also has a delete link. Open it and confirm
                to delete the paste and any attachment for good. Keep the delete link to yourself, since anyone who
                has it can delete the paste. It's only shown once, so if you lose it, the paste stays until it
                expires or reaches its view limit.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("preview", "Why don't link previews show what's in my paste?") %}
            <p class="mb-0">On purpose. Chat apps and social networks only ever see a generic "Encrypted Paste" card,
                because the server has nothing else to show them. Paste pages are also hidden from search engines.</p>
            {% endcall %}

            {% call faq("tools", "What are the tools?") %}
            <p class="mb-0">Free <a href="/tools/password">password tools</a> and
                <a href="/tools/strings">text tools</a>, such as a password generator, a strength checker, Base64 and
                hex converters and a character counter. Like pastes, they run entirely in your browser.</p>
            {% endcall %}
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
{% endblock %}
